<!--
  The Circle AI assistant, in C#.

  THIS IS THE PRODUCT, NOT A SAMPLE'S HELPER LIBRARY. A developer who writes C#
  references this and has the assistant: the capabilities, the turn contracts,
  what it remembers, what it says out loud and how it decides what was asked of
  it. They should never need to open another language, or mine a sample app for
  the parts that make it work.

  ZERO ProjectReferences, and that is still the point. This is loaded by a
  WebAssembly client as well as by a phone, so it holds data, interfaces and pure
  decisions: nothing here touches ONNX, a native library, or a file. A head
  supplies the rest by implementing these interfaces.
-->
<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <PackageId>CircleAI.Assistant</PackageId>
    <Description>The Circle AI assistant in C# — capabilities, turn contracts, what it remembers and how it decides what was asked of it. Zero project references: data, interfaces and pure decisions only, so it loads in a browser as well as on a phone. A head supplies the rest by implementing these interfaces.</Description>

    <TargetFrameworks>net9.0;net10.0</TargetFrameworks>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <RootNamespace>CircleAI.Assistant</RootNamespace>

    <!-- PACKABLE, BECAUSE THE COMMENT ABOVE IS ONLY TRUE IF IT IS. This said
         false for as long as it claimed to be the product a developer
         references — which left exactly one way to get the assistant: mine the
         sample app, the thing the header says nobody should ever have to do.
         169 sibling packages shipped the parts while the assistant itself had
         no distributable form. -->
    <IsPackable>true</IsPackable>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>false</GenerateDocumentationFile>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Tells the analyser to flag anything a browser cannot run, here, at build
         time - rather than in somebody's browser. -->
    <SupportedPlatform Include="browser" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      THE CONVERSATION STORE, AND FLUXOR CORE ONLY.

      Not Fluxor.Blazor.Web: the store, the reducers and the effect that carries
      a finished exchange through to long-term memory have no Blazor in them at
      all - they are a state machine over a conversation, and they were sitting
      in a Razor library purely because that is where they were written. A head
      with no screen needs them just as much.

      MIT, pure managed, and it loads in a browser - which is the bar everything
      in this assembly has to clear. It is still a dependency imposed on anyone
      referencing the assistant, and that is the price of the store being part
      of the product rather than part of one app.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Fluxor" Version="6.9.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
